---
/**
 * Five steps from "what is a policy" to "mine runs in three languages".
 *
 * **Every code sample here is imported from the file it lives in** with Vite's
 * `?raw`, and none is pasted (concept.md §13.6). That is deliberate and it is
 * the only thing keeping a tutorial honest over time: a pasted sample is a copy
 * that rots silently the first time someone edits the original, and a reader
 * who follows a rotted tutorial concludes the whole project is careless. Here a
 * sample cannot disagree with the code, because it *is* the code, and moving a
 * file breaks the build instead of the reader.
 *
 * The same rule covers step four's example policy: the runner loads the same
 * file the page displays, so what you read is what you watch run.
 */
import CodePanel from "../../components/CodePanel.astro";
import CompareRunner from "../../islands/CompareRunner";
import StarButton from "../../islands/StarButton";
import PolicyRunner from "../../islands/PolicyRunner";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

// The samples. Each one is a real file; none of this text is written twice.
import cacheInterface from "../../../../../packages/core/src/domains/cache/interface.ts?raw";
import lruSource from "../../../../../policies/cache/lru/index.ts?raw";
import lruPolicyJson from "../../../../../policies/cache/lru/policy.json?raw";
import evictNewestSource from "../../tutorial/evict-newest.ts?raw";

// The example policy and LRU themselves, to measure what step four claims.
import EvictNewest from "../../tutorial/evict-newest";
import Lru from "../../../../../policies/cache/lru/index";
import { createSimulation } from "../../lib/simulation";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

/**
 * What EvictNewest actually scores, measured here rather than typed.
 *
 * The page makes a claim with numbers in it, and numbers in prose are the
 * classic thing that rots. These are four cache replays — about ten
 * milliseconds at build time — so the sentence below cannot be wrong for the
 * same reason the code samples cannot be wrong.
 */
function hitRate(build: (params: Record<string, unknown>) => unknown, trace: string): number {
  const simulation = createSimulation("cache", build, { capacity: 1_000 }, trace);
  simulation.seek(simulation.totalSteps);
  return simulation.frame().metrics["hitRate"] ?? 0;
}

const NEWEST = (params: Record<string, unknown>) =>
  new EvictNewest(params as { capacity: number });
const LRU = (params: Record<string, unknown>) => new Lru(params as { capacity: number });

const scores = {
  steadyNewest: hitRate(NEWEST, "zipf-1.0-100k"),
  steadyLru: hitRate(LRU, "zipf-1.0-100k"),
  shiftingNewest: hitRate(NEWEST, "shifting-popularity"),
  shiftingLru: hitRate(LRU, "shifting-popularity"),
};
const steadyGap = scores.steadyLru - scores.steadyNewest;
const shiftingGap = scores.shiftingLru - scores.shiftingNewest;

/**
 * How much wider the gap gets, as a multiple.
 *
 * Computed rather than written, like everything else here — "a thousand times"
 * is a number in prose, and a number in prose is a thing that goes quietly
 * wrong. Guarded because a steady gap of exactly zero is possible in principle.
 */
const widening = steadyGap > 0 ? Math.round(shiftingGap / steadyGap) : null;
---

<Base
  title="Tutorial · policybook"
  description="Five steps from what a decision policy is to one you have written yourself, verified and ported to three languages."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="crumb"><a href={base}>policybook</a> / <span>tutorial</span></p>

    <article class="prose tutorial">
      <h1>Write a policy</h1>

      <!-- 1 ------------------------------------------------------------- -->
      <section id="step-1">
        <h2>1. What a policy is</h2>

        <p>
          A <strong>decision policy</strong> is a small piece of code that answers
          one question, repeatedly, with incomplete information. Which cached item
          should I drop? Should this request go through? How long before I retry?
          Which parts of this context can I forget?
        </p>
        <p>
          What the four have in common is the shape of the problem, not the
          subject. Each sees a stream of events, keeps some state, and must
          commit to an answer <em>now</em>, with no knowledge of what is coming
          next. That last constraint is the whole difficulty. A policy that could
          see the future would be easy to write and impossible to deploy, which
          is exactly what
          <a href={`${base}p/cache/opt/`}>Bélády's OPT</a> is: a bound, not a
          candidate.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every policy in this registry is described by a <code>policy.json</code>
          that the catalog validates. Nothing on this site is hand-maintained
          alongside the code. It is all read from files like this one.
        </p>

        <CodePanel code={lruPolicyJson} file="policies/cache/lru/policy.json" lang="json" />
      </section>

      <!-- 2 ------------------------------------------------------------- -->
      <section id="step-2">
        <h2>2. Read the interface</h2>

        <p>
          Each domain has one interface, and every policy in it implements
          exactly that. Here is the whole of the cache one: two required methods
          and an optional third.
        </p>

        <CodePanel
          code={cacheInterface}
          file="packages/core/src/domains/cache/interface.ts"
          anchor={{ from: "export interface CachePolicy", to: "admit?(key: K" }}
        />

        <p>
          Note what is <em>not</em> here. There is no <code>get</code>, no
          <code>put</code>, and no storage of values. The harness owns the cache
          contents while the policy owns only the decision. That separation is why
          a policy is a few dozen lines rather than a data structure library, and
          why the same policy can be implemented in TypeScript, Python and C
          against one set of test vectors.
        </p>
        <p>
          Here is LRU implementing it. Two methods, an intrusive doubly-linked
          list, and no allocation once it is warm.
        </p>

        <CodePanel
          code={lruSource}
          file="policies/cache/lru/index.ts"
          anchor={{ from: "onAccess(key: K, hit: boolean)", to: "Lru: evict() called with nothing resident" }}
        />
      </section>

      <!-- 3 ------------------------------------------------------------- -->
      <section id="step-3">
        <h2>3. Run one</h2>

        <p>
          This is that same file, running in your browser on a generated trace.
          Not a reimplementation for display: the runner imports
          <code>policies/cache/lru/index.ts</code>, the one you just read, and the
          benchmark numbers on
          <a href={`${base}p/cache/lru/`}>LRU's page</a> come from it too.
        </p>
        <p>
          Press play. The grid is the cache, in the order LRU holds it, and the
          line beneath is the running hit rate against the best any policy could
          have done knowing the whole future.
        </p>

        <PolicyRunner
          client:visible
          urlState={false}
          domain="cache"
          policies={["lru"]}
          traces={["zipf-1.0-100k", "scan-heavy"]}
          names={{ lru: "LRU" }}
          metrics={[
            { key: "hitRate", label: "Hit rate" },
            { key: "evictions", label: "Evictions" },
          ]}
          explainer="Each step is one request. The policy is told whether it was a hit, and on a miss it chooses which key to drop once the cache is full."
        />

        <p class="muted">
          Try <code>scan-heavy</code>. LRU loses its whole working set to a scan
          it will never see again, which is the failure that most of the other
          cache policies exist to avoid.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4 ------------------------------------------------------------- -->
      <section id="step-4">
        <h2>4. Write your own</h2>

        <p>Start with the scaffold, which writes all four files for you:</p>

        <CodePanel
          code={`$ pnpm policybook new cache/my-policy

  policies/cache/my-policy/policy.json     metadata the catalog validates
  policies/cache/my-policy/index.ts        the implementation
  policies/cache/my-policy/vectors.gen.ts  hand-written expectations
  policies/cache/my-policy/README.md       the explainer page`}
          file="pnpm policybook new"
          lang="bash"
          command
        />

        <p>
          Then write the simplest thing that satisfies the interface. Here is one
          that does, badly and on purpose. When the cache is full it throws out
          whatever arrived most recently, which means it spends every eviction
          undoing the insert that caused it.
        </p>

        <CodePanel
          code={evictNewestSource}
          file="apps/web/src/tutorial/evict-newest.ts"
          anchor={{ from: "export default class EvictNewest", to: "sizeOf(): number" }}
        />

        <p>
          Predict what happens before you press play. The cache fills with the
          first thousand distinct keys and then never changes again, so the hit
          rate is simply how often the trace asks for one of those.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now the part worth slowing down for. On the everyday Zipf trace,
          EvictNewest scores{" "}
          <strong class="tabular">{scores.steadyNewest.toFixed(4)}</strong> against
          LRU's <strong class="tabular">{scores.steadyLru.toFixed(4)}</strong>, a
          gap of <strong class="tabular">{steadyGap.toFixed(4)}</strong>. A policy
          designed to be as bad as possible is, on the workload most people would
          reach for first, indistinguishable from the one every textbook teaches.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is not that EvictNewest is fine. It is that a stationary trace
          cannot tell the two apart: when the popular keys never change, holding
          the first thousand of them is very nearly the right answer. Switch to{" "}
          <code>shifting-popularity</code> and the same policy scores{" "}
          <strong class="tabular">{scores.shiftingNewest.toFixed(4)}</strong>{" "}
          against <strong class="tabular">{scores.shiftingLru.toFixed(4)}</strong>,
          a gap of{" "}
          <strong class="tabular">{shiftingGap.toFixed(4)}</strong>
          {widening !== null && (
            <>, about <strong>{widening.toLocaleString("en-US")}×</strong> wider</>
          )}.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the most useful thing on this page. A benchmark can only find a
          difference its workload contains, so the first question about any
          result is not "which policy won" but "would this trace have noticed if
          the answer were wrong". Try both:
        </p>

        <CompareRunner
          client:visible
          urlState={false}
          domain="cache"
          policies={["tutorial/evict-newest", "lru"]}
          traces={["zipf-1.0-100k", "shifting-popularity"]}
          names={{ "tutorial/evict-newest": "EvictNewest", lru: "LRU" }}
          metrics={[
            { key: "hitRate", label: "Hit rate" },
            { key: "evictions", label: "Evictions" },
          ]}
          explainer="Both policies see the same requests in the same order. EvictNewest drops whatever just arrived, so its cache freezes after the first thousand distinct keys."
          seriesLabel="hit rate so far"
          referenceLabel="offline optimum"
        />

        <p class="muted">
          EvictNewest's frozen cache keeps answering yesterday's question. A
          policy that cannot forget is as broken as one that forgets everything,
          but you only find out on a workload that changes its mind.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5 ------------------------------------------------------------- -->
      <section id="step-5">
        <h2>5. Verify it, then port it</h2>

        <p>
          One command checks a policy against everything the registry expects of
          it: the metadata schema, the vectors in every language that implements
          it, and the invariants the harness enforces.
        </p>

        <CodePanel
          code={`$ pnpm policybook verify cache/my-policy

  policy.json      valid
  vectors          31 cases, 118 expectations
  typescript       31/31 pass
  python           31/31 pass
  c                31/31 pass
  invariants       evict() returned a resident key in every case`}
          file="pnpm policybook verify"
          lang="bash"
          command
        />

        <p>
          Porting is then a matter of making the same vectors pass. The Python
          and C implementations read the same <code>vectors.json</code>, so there
          is no second definition of correct to keep in sync. If all three pass,
          all three agree, and CI runs them on every commit.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the whole loop: an interface, an implementation, vectors that
          pin it, and a benchmark that says what it costs. Everything on this site
          is generated from those four things.
        </p>

        <p class="next">
          <a class="cta" href={`${base}compare/cache/`}>Compare the real ones →</a>
          <a href={`${base}d/cache/`}>or browse the cache policies</a>
        </p>

        <p class="next">
          <StarButton client:visible />
          <span class="muted">
            If this was useful, a star helps other people find it.
          </span>
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .crumb {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .tutorial section {
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  .next {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }
</style>
